{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"tid-1","run_id":"run-1","tool_use_id":"toolu-1","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-tmp/-tmp-work-r7-budget-during-call-1/sess-1/tasks/tid-1.output","summary":"**The Keeper of Hollow Point**\n\nFor thirty-one years, Elin Marr climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Hollow Point every evening at dusk, and for thirty-one years the light had never once gone dark on her watch.\n\nShe was not sentimental about it. The lamp was a machine, she told the visiting inspectors, and machines needed winding, polishing, and a steady hand on the brass. Sentiment was for postcards. But she kept a logbook in neat columns, and in the margins, small enough that no inspector ever bothered to read them, she wrote things like *fog thick as wool, saw a gull sleeping on the water* and *the boy from the mainland waved today, I waved back.*\n\nThe boy was Tomas Haldane, the ferryman's son, who rowed out with supplies on Thursdays. He was eleven when she first noticed him, sitting on the rocks below the tower with a sketchbook. By the next spring he was bringing her charcoal. By the autumn after that he was asking questions about the Fresnel lens, the way the prisms bent a single flame into a beam that could reach twenty miles out to sea.\n\n\"Why does it turn?\" he asked once, lying on his back on the gallery floor.\n\n\"So ships can tell us apart from the stars,\" Elin said. \"A steady light means a rock. A flashing light means a harbor. A light that comes and goes means someone is trying very hard to be found.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a long time after that.\n\nThe storm came in the third week of November. Elin had seen a hundred storms, but this one arrived sideways, the sea climbing the cliffs like it had somewhere to be. The wind screamed through the gallery railings. At midnight the lamp flickered, a dying sort of stutter, and she realized the oil feed had cracked in the cold.\n\nShe could fix it. It would take an hour, maybe two, with her hands in freezing brass and the tower shuddering around her. She went down to the store room for the spare gasket and found the cabinet already open.\n\nTomas stood there, soaked to the skin, holding a lantern in one hand and the gasket in the other.\n\n\"Your father is going to kill you,\" she said.\n\n\"He's asleep. I told him I was checking the boat.\" He swallowed. \"There's a fishing schooner out past the Teeth. I saw its lights from the cove. It was drifting toward the rocks, and then the lamp went out, and I thought—\"\n\nHe didn't finish. He didn't have to.\n\nThey worked together in the lamp room with the wind hammering the glass. Elin's fingers went numb, then useless, and Tomas did the fine work, threading the gasket with a patience she hadn't expected from a boy his age. When the flame finally caught and swelled, bright and clean, he let out a breath that fogged the glass.\n\nOut past the Teeth, a small red light answered theirs, then another, then a third, as the schooner's crew found their bearings and turned for the harbor.\n\nElin wrote in the logbook before dawn. *Storm, northeast gale. Oil feed cracked, repaired with help. Three vessels returned to harbor. Tomas Haldane present and of great use.* Then, in the margin, smaller than usual: *I am getting old. I am not sorry about it.*\n\nIn the morning, the sea lay flat and gray and innocent, as if it had never tried to kill anyone. Tomas sat on the rocks below with his sketchbook open, drawing the lighthouse from below, the great lamp crowning the tower like a held breath.\n\nElin climbed down to join him. Tomorrow, she thought, she would teach him to wind the clockwork. The day after that, the logbook.\n\nSomeone would need to keep the light burning, and she had always known it would not be her forever.","usage":{"total_tokens":6445,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":9394},"uuid":"uuid-53","session_id":"sess-1"}
